EU REACH REGULATION (EC 1907/2006) — COMPREHENSIVE SUMMARY
============================================================

OVERVIEW
---------
REACH = Registration, Evaluation, Authorisation and Restriction of Chemicals
Entered into force: 1 June 2007
Regulation (EC) No 1907/2006 of the European Parliament and of the Council
Purpose: improve protection of human health and the environment from chemical risks while enhancing competitiveness of the EU chemicals industry. Promotes alternative methods for hazard assessment to reduce animal testing.

CORE PRINCIPLES
----------------
- Burden of Proof on Industry: Companies must identify and manage risks, demonstrate safe use to ECHA, and communicate risk management measures to users
- Substitution Principle: Most hazardous substances should eventually be substituted with less dangerous ones
- "No data, no market": Substances cannot be manufactured or imported without registration data

SCOPE
------
Applies to all chemical substances manufactured or imported into the EU in quantities >= 1 tonne per year:
- Industrial chemicals
- Cleaning products, paints
- Substances in articles (clothes, furniture, electrical appliances)
Exclusions: Radioactive substances, wastes, non-isolated intermediates, certain naturally occurring substances

THE FOUR PROCESSES
-------------------

1. REGISTRATION
   - Companies must register substances with ECHA
   - Required for substances manufactured/imported at >= 1 tonne/year
   - Companies registering the same substance must cooperate (SIEF — Substance Information Exchange Forums) to share data and avoid duplicative testing
   - Registration dossier includes: technical dossier (identity, classification, guidance on safe use, study summaries) and chemical safety report (for >=10 tonnes/year)
   - Tonnage bands determine data requirements:
     * 1-10 tonnes: physicochemical, in vitro, and (if needed) screening level data
     * 10-100 tonnes: Level 1 data including developmental toxicity, toxicokinetics
     * 100-1000 tonnes: Level 2 data including 2-generation reproductive toxicity, chronic toxicity
     * >=1000 tonnes: Full data package including carcinogenicity
   - Late pre-registration possible under certain conditions
   - Phase-in substances registered in three deadlines: 2010, 2013, 2018

2. EVALUATION
   - ECHA evaluates individual registration dossiers for compliance (compliance check)
   - EU Member States evaluate selected substances to clarify specific health or environmental concerns (substance evaluation)
   - Evaluation may request additional information from registrants
   - Community Rolling Action Plan (CoRAP): Member State Committee prioritizes substances for evaluation
   - Examination of testing proposals: ECHA reviews proposals for animal tests before they proceed

3. AUTHORISATION
   - For Substances of Very High Concern (SVHC) listed in Annex XIV
   - SVHC criteria: CMR (Cat 1A/1B), PBT/vPvB, or equivalent concern (e.g., endocrine disruptors)
   - After a "sunset date," substances cannot be used or placed on the market without specific authorisation
   - Authorisation granted if: risk adequately controlled (adequate control route) OR socio-economic benefits outweigh risks and no suitable alternatives (socio-economic route)
   - Applicants must analyze alternatives and submit substitution plans
   - Authorisation is time-limited and reviewed periodically

4. RESTRICTION
   - For unacceptable risks to human health or the environment that need EU-wide action
   - Restrictions in Annex XVII can ban, limit, or impose conditions on manufacture, use, or placing on the market
   - Any substance (on its own, in mixture, or in article) can be restricted
   - Restriction is the "safety net" — applies to all substances not already covered by other EU legislation
   - Proposals can come from Member States or ECHA (at request of European Commission)

ECHA'S ROLE
-----------
- European Chemicals Agency, based in Helsinki
- Manages registration database and IT systems (REACH-IT)
- Coordinates evaluation process
- Scientific committees (RAC — Risk Assessment Committee, SEAC — Socio-Economic Analysis Committee)
- Forum for Exchange of Information on Enforcement
- Maintains public database of chemical information (EUCLEF)
- Manages public consultations for regulatory processes

IMPACT ON COMPANIES
--------------------
- Manufacturer: makes chemicals for own use or supply (even for export)
- Importer: buys anything from outside EU/EEA (individual chemicals, mixtures, or finished products)
- Downstream User: handles chemicals in industrial or professional activities
- Non-EU Company: not directly bound by REACH; responsibility transfers to EU importer or appointed Only Representative in EU

DOWNSTREAM USER OBLIGATIONS
-----------------------------
- Must check if substances are registered for their uses
- Must apply risk management measures from extended SDS
- Must notify ECHA if using substance in way not covered by supplier's registration
- May prepare own Chemical Safety Report for uses not covered

DATA SHARING
-------------
- Mandatory joint submission of data (per EU 2016/9)
- Data-sharing post-deadline provisions (EU 2019/1692)
- Cost-sharing mechanisms
- Test Methods Regulation (EC 440/2008) updated for technical progress

RELATED EU CHEMICALS LEGISLATION
----------------------------------
- CLP Regulation (EC 1272/2008): Classification, Labelling, Packaging
- BPR (EU 528/2012): Biocidal Products Regulation
- PIC (EU 649/2012): Prior Informed Consent
- POPs Regulation: Persistent Organic Pollutants
- OELs: Occupational Exposure Limits directives
- Water Framework Directive
- RoHS: Restriction of Hazardous Substances
